According to recent data, 337 food recalls have already been issued this year, compared to a record of 695 all-time. This highlights a concerning acceleration in food safety issues, with 91 of these being the most severe Class I recalls. Among the culprits are undeclared allergens, foreign objects, and an unlikely yet concerning offender: unapproved food dyes.

Red No. 40, Yellow No. 5, and Yellow No. 6 are some of the usual suspects that have graced ingredients lists, but what happens when they're not on the label? As was the case with the *52USA brand Popping Boba*, where tartrazine, sunset yellow, and allura red made unwelcome appearances. These dyes, while permitted under specific conditions in the United States, require declaration on packaging.

Another eyebrow-raiser is the Ganesha Enterprises LLC recall of certain lots of Kesar Mukhwas due to undeclared and unapproved food coloring, including Acid Red 14. For the uninitiated, this is not what you want in your food. It's worth noting that products using unapproved colors are typically not subject to the same rigorous testing as those following established guidelines.

While recalls over coloring may seem more of a regulatory headache than a health emergency, it's a reminder that proper labeling and compliance with food safety laws are paramount. Incorrectly labeled products can cause severe allergic reactions or other health implications for sensitive individuals.

Brands with repeated recalls, such as CHETAK NEW YORK LLC, Made Fresh Salads Inc, and Diva Fam Inc, further illustrate a systemic issue in the manufacturing pipeline. Trust is earned through consistency in quality and safety, not by cutting corners.

When it comes to food safety, don't assume colorful packaging and ingredients mean a brand has done its due diligence. As recalls mount, it's clear that consumers must double-check labels and stay informed through trusted channels.
